## An agent interface can extend the app you already have

For a small software business, agent support can be an extension of a useful feature rather than a commitment to rebuild the product around chat. Gradio’s new image-workflow example shows how: someone can edit an image in a browser, while an assistant can call the same operation as part of a larger assignment. [Rebuilding AUTOMATIC1111 with Gradio Workflow (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/blog/gradio-workflow-1111).

The workflow supplies both the visual interface and callable outputs, including tools exposed through MCP, the protocol many assistants use to access software. That leaves a common operation to maintain even when the callers differ. Authentication still needs custom integration in this example; generating interfaces does not finish the engineering. [Rebuilding AUTOMATIC1111 with Gradio Workflow (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/blog/gradio-workflow-1111), [Workflow1111 app.py at pinned public commit (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/spaces/ysharma/Workflow1111/raw/5ff721fbf5e19bb32ace470bea6fa5be438210aa/app.py).

A customer could learn a feature by using it directly, then delegate the repeated work once satisfied with its results. That is a plausible route to adoption for existing products: preserve the controls people understand, while letting their agents incorporate the underlying capability into a wider workflow. The agent becomes another customer of the software. [Rebuilding AUTOMATIC1111 with Gradio Workflow (Hugging Face)](https://huggingface.co/blog/gradio-workflow-1111).

## What can the crew do while the robot works?

An automation product becomes more valuable when a crew can hand over a stretch of work and get on with something else. A machine that produces faster while keeping the same people occupied offers a narrower benefit. That distinction matters when judging what better AI perception actually buys. [Where Has Construction Automation Been Successful? (Construction Physics)](https://www.construction-physics.com/p/where-has-construction-automation).

Construction Physics’s survey describes a bricklaying robot that needed a fresh setup at each wall. Masons also followed it to finish mortar joints and straighten bricks. The author argues that adaptable AI could overcome historical constraints like these. But learning to navigate a corner and eliminating the finishing work are separate achievements; improving the first does not establish the second. [Where Has Construction Automation Been Successful? (Construction Physics)](https://www.construction-physics.com/p/where-has-construction-automation).

For a contractor, the useful test would be whether the improved machine lets a mason take another assignment, or changes what that mason watches and fixes. That gives developers a commercial target: extend the stretch of work a crew can safely delegate. More adaptable behavior could help, provided it reduces the crew’s obligations around the machine as well as its interruptions. [Where Has Construction Automation Been Successful? (Construction Physics)](https://www.construction-physics.com/p/where-has-construction-automation).
